course: dictionary
lesson: 2090
title: Containers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A container is anything you can keep or carry things in, such as a box, jar, bottle, tin or tub. It is a noun, so you can have one container or many containers.
explain: The clues in «You should never taste, sniff or drink anything from an unknown bottle or container.» show what a container does. A bottle and a jar both hold things inside them, so a container is something that holds things.
explain: Some containers are huge. A shipping container is a very large metal box for carrying goods on ships, trains and lorries. In «One big ship can carry about 20,000 containers», the size of the ship tells you the containers are big too.
fact: A container is anything you can keep or carry things in.
fact: "Container" is a noun, and its plural is "containers".
fact: A shipping container is a very large metal box for carrying goods on ships, trains and lorries.
fact: Liquids take the shape of their container, and a gas spreads out to fill any container it is in.
fact: In maths, containers come up in capacity problems: how much a container holds, in litres or millilitres.
exercise: «In bear country, store food in bear-proof containers.» What is a container? | A kind of animal that lives in the woods | A type of sleeping bag | A loud noise made by a bear | *Anything you can keep or carry things in
exercise: «A gas spreads out to fill any container it is in.» Why does a gas fill the whole container? | Gases are always heavy and sink to the bottom | Containers are always empty inside | Gas is made of tiny bricks | *Its particles are far apart and fly about fast
exercise: Which one is a shipping container? | *A very large metal box for carrying goods on ships and lorries | A bottle for water | A glass jar for jam | A small plastic tub for lunch
test: «Most goods travel by ship, and one big ship can carry about 20,000 containers.» What does a supply chain mean? | A list of everything in a shop | *All the steps that bring goods to you | A chain used to tie containers to a ship | A long line of people waiting to buy
test: In a science lesson, you pour water into a tall glass and then into a wide bowl. Why does the water change shape? | The bowl is heavier than the glass | Water breaks into tiny pieces when it is poured | *Liquids take the shape of their container | Water is always the same shape, so it does not change
exam: «They keep all food in a bear-proof container away from the tents.» What does container mean here? | A kind of bear | A shop that sells food | A tent for sleeping in | *A box or tub that holds food safely
